            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="HLVCjnMXgUOP8Id3NHjwa0" Bounds="18,34,446,351" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="This patch is similar to the first one. The main difference is the additional section that demonstrates how to save a sequence of frames into a folder so they can later be imported into Adobe Premiere and assembled into a movie.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;As is often the case in vvvv, there are multiple ways to achieve the same result. However, the underlying principle remains the same: an image is rendered and then saved to the hard drive. The challenge is that vvvv typically runs at 60 frames per second, and it is not always possible for the computer to write images to the hard drive at that speed. Depending on the hardware and image resolution, it may or may not keep up.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;To address this, each frame is first stored in a continuously growing list. The &quot;Queue&quot;  node performs this task, and its maximum length can be defined by the user. Once you are satisfied with the animation, the stored frames can be written to the hard drive at a controlled pace. This approach avoids dropped frames and ensures that the complete animation can be saved reliably.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
